Local patches to tads2/tads3 base code
=======================================

doc/SRC_GUIDELINES says the tads2/ and tads3/ directories are read-only
and should not be modified locally, since any local changes are silently
lost the next time FrobTADS is re-synced with a new upstream TADS base
code release. This directory exists because one feature (UTF-8 support
in the ncurses interface, plus the "ansi" interface) genuinely could not
be implemented without touching a few functions in tads2/osgen3.c and
tads2/osgen.h - see "Why these can't live in src/ alone" below.

This is the same kind of exception the maintainer has made before for
build/portability fixes that were blocking the port (e.g. commits
6fa459a, d368a8d, 110c94d, 52c09ea), just for a feature rather than a
build fix. Per SRC_GUIDELINES' own spirit ("problems... should only be
reported, not fixed"), these fixes should ideally also be reported
upstream (to the TADS maintainer) rather than living here indefinitely.


What's here
===========

frobtads-utf8-ansi-basecode.patch
    The full diff against tads2/osgen.h and tads2/osgen3.c introduced by
    the UTF-8/ncurses fixes and the "ansi" interface. Generated with:

        git diff <base-code-sync-commit>..<tip> -- tads2/

    See doc/UTF8_NCURSES for a full explanation of what these changes
    fix and why.


Why these can't live in src/ alone
===================================

Most of the affected logic lives in functions that are either `static`
(file-private to osgen3.c, with no linker-visible symbol a porter could
override) or are the *only* implementation of a given os_* interface
function that TADS2's build model expects to link against exactly once
(os_print, os_set_text_color, os_set_text_attr, os_set_screen_color).
tads2/osgen3.c's own header comment documents a real extension-point
mechanism for this (the USE_STDIO / USE_STATLINE / etc. macros that
os_plain() itself relies on), but it operates at the level of "opt in or
out of this entire subsystem", not "replace just these three functions
and keep the rest". The only way to avoid touching osgen3.c at all would
be to stop using its console/line-editor/scrollback subsystem entirely
and hand-write a from-scratch replacement of comparable scope (in
practice, upwards of 1500 lines) in src/.


How to re-apply after a base-code sync
=======================================

After FrobTADS is re-synced with a new upstream TADS release (i.e.
tads2/ and tads3/ are replaced wholesale), re-apply with:

    git apply patches/frobtads-utf8-ansi-basecode.patch

If it fails to apply cleanly (likely, since upstream TADS has presumably
changed in the meantime), use it as a reference for manually re-porting
the same logic into the new osgen3.c/osgen.h - the patch's comments
explain the intent of each change, not just the mechanics.
